    id: 'blog-mcp-ai-publishing',
    slug: 'autonomous-ai-publishing-with-mcp-and-scoped-keys',
    title:
      'Designing Resilient AI Publishing Agents with Model Context Protocol (MCP) & Scoped Keys',
    excerpt:
      'How we built a secure stdio and HTTP Model Context Protocol (MCP) publishing server that allows AI coding assistants to draft, review, and schedule CMS articles safely.',
    content:
      "# Designing Resilient AI Publishing Agents with Model Context Protocol (MCP) & Scoped Keys\n\nThe emergence of the **Model Context Protocol (MCP)** by Anthropic and open ecosystem tooling has enabled AI assistants (like Claude, Cursor, and custom agent loops) to move from passive autocomplete to active pair programmers capable of calling structured API tools directly.\n\nIn this article, we explore the architecture of our `@portfolio/mcp` server, which enables autonomous drafting, content auditing, and publishing while maintaining strict authorization boundaries.\n\n![AI Agent Automation Interface](https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1677442136019-21780ecad995?auto=format&fit=crop&w=1200&q=80)\n\n---\n\n## 1. The MCP Tool Contract\n\nAn MCP server exposes standardized tools via JSON-RPC over `stdio` or SSE. Our server exports tools for:\n- `draft_blog`: Creates a draft article with markdown body and tags.\n- `update_blog`: Modifies an existing draft with optimistic concurrency locking.\n- `schedule_blog`: Enqueues an article for automatic publication at a designated future instant.\n- `list_media`: Queries available image assets in Supabase Storage.\n\n```typescript\n// MCP Tool Registration Definition\nexport const draftBlogTool = {\n  name: 'draft_blog',\n  description: 'Drafts a new blog post in the portfolio CMS',\n  inputSchema: {\n    type: 'object',\n    properties: {\n      title: { type: 'string', description: 'Article title' },\n      content: { type: 'string', description: 'Full Markdown content' },\n      tags: { type: 'array', items: { type: 'string' } },\n      excerpt: { type: 'string' },\n    },\n    required: ['title', 'content', 'tags'],\n  },\n};\n```\n\n![Security Architecture Flow](https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1563986768609-322da13575f3?auto=format&fit=crop&w=1200&q=80)\n\n---\n\n## 2. Scoped API Key Authentication (`pf_live_*`)\n\nGiving an autonomous agent full admin credentials is a massive security hazard. Instead, we issue scoped API keys prefixed with `pf_live_`:\n- **Cryptographic Hashing**: Keys are generated with 256 bits of entropy and stored in the database exclusively as Argon2/SHA-256 hashes.\n- **Granular Scopes**: `blogs:draft`, `blogs:publish`, `media:upload`, `projects:read`.\n- **Automatic Audit Trail**: Every write operation records the exact Actor ID, timestamp, IP address, and changed fields.\n\n```typescript\n// API Scope Guard in NestJS\n@Injectable()\nexport class ScopeGuard implements CanActivate {\n  constructor(private reflector: Reflector) {}\n\n  canActivate(context: ExecutionContext): boolean {\n    const requiredScopes = this.reflector.get<string[]>('scopes', context.getHandler());\n    const request = context.switchToHttp().getRequest();\n    const key = request.publisherKey;\n\n    if (!key || !requiredScopes.every((scope) => key.scopes.includes(scope))) {\n      throw new ForbiddenException('Insufficient publisher key permissions.');\n    }\n    return true;\n  }\n}\n```\n\n![AI Agent Workflow Diagram](https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1618005182384-a83a8bd57fbe?auto=format&fit=crop&w=1200&q=80)\n\n---\n\n## 3. Human-in-the-Loop Safety\n\nEven with autonomous drafting capabilities, our publishing protocol mandates:\n1. All AI-originated content is marked with `createdByAI: true`.\n2. Content defaults to `DRAFT` status.\n3. Final publication requires either explicit admin approval or pre-authorized scheduled release times.\n\nThis architecture delivers high velocity while guaranteeing that human editorial standards and security controls are never bypassed.",
