    id: 'blog-media-pipelines',
    slug: 'zero-egress-media-pipelines-supabase-storage',
    title:
      'Zero-Egress Media Architecture with Supabase Storage, Sharp & Next/Image',
    excerpt:
      'How to build high-performance image upload and transformation pipelines using presigned S3 URLs, edge CDN caching, and zero-egress architecture.',
    content:
      "# Zero-Egress Media Architecture with Supabase Storage, Sharp & Next/Image\n\nHandling user media uploads incorrectly can quickly ruin application performance and balloon cloud infrastructure bills. Streaming multi-megabyte image uploads through an API server consumes memory, ties up Node.js event loops, and incurs double egress costs (Client → API Server → S3 Storage Bucket).\n\nIn this article, we examine how we engineered a zero-egress media pipeline using Supabase Storage (S3-compatible API), Sharp image optimization, and Next.js `<Image>` edge transformations.\n\n![Cloud Storage and Media Infrastructure](https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1544383835-bda2bc66a55d?auto=format&fit=crop&w=1200&q=80)\n\n---\n\n## 1. Direct Presigned Browser Uploads\n\nThe core philosophy of zero-egress architecture is simple: **the application server never handles raw image bytes**.\n\nInstead, the browser requests a cryptographically signed upload ticket from the backend and transfers the binary payload directly to object storage over HTTPS.\n\n```\n1. Browser → POST /api/storage/presign { filename: 'hero.png', mime: 'image/png' }\n2. API Server → Returns presigned S3 upload URL with 60-second expiration.\n3. Browser → Direct PUT request to Supabase S3 bucket.\n4. Browser → POST /api/media/confirm { storagePath, alt, width, height }\n```\n\n```typescript\n// Presign route handler in NestJS\n@Post('storage/presign')\n@UseGuards(JwtAuthGuard)\nasync getPresignedUploadUrl(@Body() dto: PresignDto) {\n  const extension = dto.filename.split('.').pop()?.toLowerCase() || 'webp';\n  const uniqueKey = `blogs/${dto.slug}/${uuidv4()}.${extension}`;\n\n  const { data, error } = await this.supabase.storage\n    .from('portfolio-storage')\n    .createSignedUploadUrl(uniqueKey);\n\n  if (error) throw new InternalServerErrorException(error.message);\n  return { signedUrl: data.signedUrl, path: data.path };\n}\n```\n\n![Image Transformation Processing](https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1460925895917-afdab827c52f?auto=format&fit=crop&w=1200&q=80)\n\n---\n\n## 2. Client-Side Pre-Processing & WebP Compression\n\nBefore initiating an upload, high-resolution camera photos (often 12MB+ JPEGs) are normalized in a browser Web Worker using the OffscreenCanvas API:\n- Images are scaled down to a maximum width of **2400 pixels**.\n- Unnecessary EXIF metadata (GPS coordinates, camera serials) is stripped.\n- Images are encoded to WebP at 85% quality, reducing payload size by **75% to 90%** before transmission.\n\n![Edge CDN Distribution Map](https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1558494949-ef010cbdcc31?auto=format&fit=crop&w=1200&q=80)\n\n---\n\n## 3. Global Edge CDN Delivery & Next.js `<Image>`\n\nOnce stored in the bucket, images are delivered through a global CDN with `Cache-Control: public, max-age=31536000, immutable`. Next.js transforms assets on demand, generating responsive `srcset` variants in modern **AVIF** and **WebP** formats with automatic blur placeholders.\n\nThis setup achieves **100/100 Google Lighthouse media performance scores** while keeping server compute costs virtually at zero.",
